<!--META-DATA
{
	tags: for, indexing,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	Given the absolute index of the game state data matrix, this program returns the corresponding (x,y) coordinate on the tic-tac-toe interface. This is just the reverse direction of the previous program.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>abs_index</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer. The absolute index of the corresponding value on <pf>game_state.data</pf>.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>x</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer. The x-coordinate of the bottom left corner of a space.</p>
			<p><pf>y</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer. The y-coordinate of the bottom left corner of a space.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>As in the previous problem, you can hardcode this program specifically for the tic-tac-toe game. That is,</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>the only absolute indices that your code needs to handle are 1-9</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>. See the previous figure for the mapping of absolute index to (x,y) coordinates</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		[x, y] = index_2_click(9)
		x = 3
		y = 1
		[x, y] = index_2_click(9)
		x = 1
		y = 2
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
